{"records":[{"id":"4fsr289sg8","title":"Mitochondrial RNA Extrusion and Its m6A-Dependent Regulation of Innate Immunity","description":"Mitochondrial RNA (mtRNA) released into the cytosol acts as a damage-associated molecular pattern (DAMP) that activates pattern-recognition receptor (PRR)-mediated inflammation. However, the mechanisms underlying mtRNA extrusion and its subsequent cytoplasmic fate remain poorly understood. Here, we established pharmacological models of mitochondrial nucleic acid extrusion and demonstrated that mtRNA release was accompanied by mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) release. Knockdown of the outer mitochondrial membrane proteins VDAC1 or BAX reduced the release of both mitochondrial nucleic acids, suggesting their involvement in this process. Extruded mtRNA induced innate immune responses in A549 cells and was recognized by multiple RNA-sensing PRRs, including RIG-I, MDA5, TLR3, TLR7, TLR8, and PKR, which contributed differentially to immune signaling. Analysis of public GEO datasets and methylated RNA immunoprecipitation (MeRIP) assays further supported the presence of N6-methyladenosine (m6A) modifications on mtRNA. Notably, m6A-associated proteins interacted with cytoplasmic mtRNA and modulated its cytoplasmic persistence and association with RIG-I, thereby influencing mtRNA-induced innate immune responses. Collectively, our findings establish experimental models for studying mitochondrial nucleic acid extrusion and identify m6A-associated regulation as a potential mechanism controlling mtRNA-mediated innate immunity, with possible implications for therapeutic intervention in inflammation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-11-06T16:31:21.426Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4fsr289sg8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4fsr289sg8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mijia","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32270148"}],"categories":["Mitochondrial RNA","Innate Immune Response","m6A Methylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xz5fx4s7x2","title":"Alteration of cardiolipin-dependent mitochondrial coupling in muscle  protects against obesity","description":"Prola et al. show that mitochondria from muscle of Hacd1 knockout mice have altered membranes lipid composition and cristae shape yielding a reduced respiratory efficiency with neither proton leak nor oxidative stress, and consequently leading to an overall increase in energy expenditure that eventually protects against a diet-induced obesity.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-05-17T13:03:26.833Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xz5fx4s7x2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xz5fx4s7x2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fanny","last_name":"Pilot-Storck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrion","Metabolism","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gwm33dyczg","title":"Data for: Vitamin D protects against oxidative stress, inflammation and hepatorenal damage induced by acute paracetamol toxicity in rat.","description":"Vitamin D protects against oxidative stress, inflammation and hepatorenal damage induced by acute paracetamol toxicity in rat.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-02T19:54:14.309Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gwm33dyczg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gwm33dyczg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bassem","last_name":"Refaat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohammad A.","last_name":"BaSalamah","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohamed","last_name":"Elzubier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohamed","last_name":"El-boshy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amani","last_name":"Mahbub","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jawwad","last_name":"Ahmad","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mazen M.","last_name":"Ghaith","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Riyad","last_name":"Almaimani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hussain","last_name":"Almasmoum","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Abdelghany","last_name":"Abdelghany","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shakir","last_name":"Idris","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Inflammation","Vitamin D","Oxidative Damage","Acetaminophen","Interleukin-10 Family","Interleukin-17 Family","Interleukin-1"],"journal_issn":["08915849"]},{"id":"7swxd2x8zx","title":"original data--Narirutin Protects Against Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Mitigating Mitochondrial Apoptosis via Inhibition of CaMKII Activation","description":"This is the original data of the article named  \"Narirutin Protects Against Myocardial Ischemia-Reperfusion Injury by Mitigating Mitochondrial Apoptosis via Inhibition of CaMKII Activation\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-04T11:40:40.729Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7swxd2x8zx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7swxd2x8zx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wangyan","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9gp32fzwtr","title":"Distinct age-related pattern of mitochondrial somatic mutations across Multiple Sclerosis phenotypes","description":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a chronic, autoimmune, inflammatory disease of the central nervous system (CNS) characterized by demyelination and neurodegeneration. Among the various pathogenic mechanisms, oxidative stress plays a critical role in driving both inflammation and neuronal damage, and emerging evidence suggests that mitochondrial dysfunction may significantly contribute to disease progression. Whole mtDNA was sequenced from blood-derived DNA using long-range PCR and Illumina® Nextera XT kit. Somatic mutations were defined based on heteroplasmy levels between 1–5%. Linear regression models were used to assess the association between age and mutation rate. We observed a significant age-dependent increase in low-frequency nonsynonymous mtDNA mutations in MS. Analyses stratified by disease course revealed this effect was driven by PPMS patients, while no association was seen in RRMS, suggesting course-specific mitochondrial trajectories. Furthermore, fast-progressing patients showed a positive linear relationship between age and mtDNA mutations rate, while slow-progressing ones showed a negative trend. These findings support the existence of a differential age-related accumulation of somatic mtDNA mutations across MS courses and underline the importance of mitochondrial genome instability in disease progression.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-08T08:38:15.282Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/9gp32fzwtr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9gp32fzwtr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Melissa","last_name":"Sorosina","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Federica","last_name":"Esposito","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Massimo","last_name":"Filippi","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00789fa95","name":"Ministero della Salute","grant_id":"GR-2019-12368672"}],"categories":["DNA","DNA Sequencing","Mitochondrial DNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"msbbtx6k9s","title":"The mitochondrial RNA extrusion-induced innate immunity is regulated by N6-methyladenosine machinery","description":"Mitochondrial RNA (mtRNA) released into the cytosol functions as a damage associated molecular pattern that activates pattern-recognition receptor (PRR)-mediated inflammation, yet its release mechanisms and cytoplasmic fate remain poorly understood. Here we report that chemical Abt-373-treatment and Vesicular stomatitis virus (VSV) infection induce mtRNA extrusion through Bax/Bak and VDAC1 channels, accompanied by mtDNA release. Extruded mtRNA in A549 cells activates multiple cytosolic PRRs, including RIG-I, MDA5, TLR3/7/8, and PKR, each contributing differentially to the innate immune signaling. Analysis of GEO datasets and methylated RNA immunoprecipitation (MeRIP) assays futher reveals that mtRNA carries methyladenosine (m6A)  modification. m6A machinery proteins are involved in the cytoplasmic retention time of mtRNA and its interaction with RIG-I, thereby modulating mtRNA-induced innate immunity. Thus, our work establishes in vitro models of mtRNA extrusion, and highlights m6A-dependent modulation as a potential therapeutic target for mtRNA-driven inflammation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-29T08:31:23.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/msbbtx6k9s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/msbbtx6k9s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mijia","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qin","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifei","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shixuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xueping","last_name":"Zeng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fengyu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ting","last_name":"Fu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yueqing","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianxing","last_name":"Lyu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrial RNA","Innate Immune Response"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yzk5bndyzt","title":"Transmission electron microscopy","description":"showed that in the control group the microstructure of the neurons was normal, mitochondria were well arranged and distributed unevenly and orderly, mitochondrial cristae were compact, with a well-defined double membrane structure without swelling. The AHH group showed significant injuries than the control group, including a remarkable cell loss and karyopyknosis, significantly decreased mitochondria that were also swollen, without cristae and with a fragmented double membrane structure. Furthermore, some channels of the endoplasmic network were atypically swollen. Crocin pretreatment significantly reduced injuries compared with the AHH group, including cell loss and karyopyknosis in hippocampal neurons, with a significantly higher number of mitochondria in all the crocin-treated groups, decreased mitochondrial swelling, and with an intact double membrane structure and cristae, suggesting a protection of the mitochondrial structure, especially in the HHG that showed the best effect","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-18T12:55:01.833Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yzk5bndyzt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yzk5bndyzt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoyan","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5jp6c2wzmz","title":"A Cold Stress Inducible PERK/OGT Axis Controls TOM70-Assisted Mitochondrial Protein Import and Cristae Formation (Latorre-Muro et al.)","description":"Raw images from \"A Cold Stress Inducible PERK/OGT Axis Controls TOM70-Assisted Mitochondrial Protein Import and Cristae Formation\" (Latorre-Muro et al.)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-17T10:51:25.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5jp6c2wzmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5jp6c2wzmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pere","last_name":"Puigserver","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Post-Transcription Modification","Mitochondrion","Endoplasmic Reticulum Stress","Mitochondrial Function"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"knj24fd366","title":"The N-degron pathway mediates the autophagic degradation of cytosolic mitochondrial DNA during sterile innate immune responses. Jung et al.","description":"The human body reacts to tissue damage by generating damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) that activate sterile immune responses. To date, little is known about how DAMPs are removed to avoid excessive immune responses. Here, we show that proteasomal dysfunction induces the release of mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) as a DAMP that activates the cGAS-STING pathway and is subsequently degraded through the N-degron pathway. In the resolution phase of sterile immune responses, DNA-PK senses cytosolic mtDNA and activates N-terminal (Nt-) arginylation by ATE1 R-transferases. The substrates of Nt-arginylation include the molecular chaperone BiP/GRP78 retrotranslocated from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). R-BiP, the Nt-arginylated species of BiP, is associated with cytosolic mtDNA to accelerate its targeting to autophagic membranes for lysosomal degradation. Thus, cytosolic mtDNA activates the N-degron pathway to facilitate its own degradation to form a negative feedback loop, by which the cell can turn off sterile immune responses in a right time.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-23T09:47:51.712Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/knj24fd366","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/knj24fd366"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chan Hoon","last_name":"Jung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoon Jee ","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eun Hye","last_name":"Cho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gee Eun","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sung Tae","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ki Sa","last_name":"Sung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daeho","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dong Hyun","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yeon Sung","last_name":"Son","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin-Hyun","last_name":"Ahn","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dohyun","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong Tae","last_name":"Kwon","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"2021R1A2B5B03002614"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"2019R1A2C1089497"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2023-00246864"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"2020R1A5A1019023"},{"id":"https://ror.org/013aysd81","name":"National Research Foundation of Korea","grant_id":"RS-2024-00410315"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"66bgm2cbyc","title":"Data for: Developmental Data for Several Human Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) Long Amplification Targets","description":"This material represents developmental data for several human mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) long amplification targets.  It was part of a larger study in which a quantitative PCR assay was designed to quantify and assess quality, including degradation, of human mtDNA","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-26T15:21:07.032Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66bgm2cbyc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66bgm2cbyc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mark","last_name":"Kavlick","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medical Science","Biological Science Tools"],"journal_issn":["23523409"]}],"total_records":119987,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":110,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":119987,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":119441,"restricted":63,"embargoed":483},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2090},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":6